WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.550
A network server faces a
constant balancing act.

2
00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.930
It has to handle incoming data from hundreds
of connections at the same time without

3
00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.080
letting a slow client
bottleneck the entire system.

4
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.040
We can measure this problem in lab TD7.

5
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:20.480
We send 32 clients to
a web server at once.

6
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.430
24 of them send the requests instantly,
but eight of them are intentionally slow,

7
00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:27.980
taking a full second to
transmit their data.

8
00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.760
We start with an iterative server model.

9
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:35.920
This setup runs on a single thread and
processes exactly one connection at a time.

10
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:39.990
While the server is busy with the first
client, the other 31 connections complete

11
00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.340
their three-way handshake.

12
00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:45.510
The kernel marks their connection as
established, but they are trapped waiting in

13
00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.620
the listening socket's accept queue.

14
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.780
This terminal output shows
the results of that delay.

15
00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:56.450
Our fast clients wait a median time of
982 milliseconds just to get a response,

16
00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:58.900
simply because they are
stuck behind the slow ones.

17
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.600
In an iterative model, the server can
only move as fast as its slowest client.

18
00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:08.640
The CPU sits idle while waiting for network
data, wasting valuable processing power.

19
00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:13.250
To clear out that queue, the server can
create a new process for every connection by

20
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.600
calling fork right after accept.

21
00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.500
This isolates the workload.

22
00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:21.810
The new child process serves the client,
which frees the parent process to instantly

23
00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.080
loop back and accept the next arrival.

24
00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.520
But this creates administrative overhead.

25
00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:31.010
The server needs a SIGCHLD handler to
reap child processes when they finish,

26
00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:33.700
preventing them from becoming
resource-draining zombies.

27
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:38.790
Process isolation keeps fast clients moving,
but creating a whole new process costs

28
00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:42.180
several times more system
memory than creating a thread.

29
00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:45.940
We can swap the child process
for a new thread per connection.

30
00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:49.640
This is cheaper to create and
takes advantage of shared memory.

31
00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.800
However, this approach hits
a hard physical limit.

32
00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:57.510
If a server gets 10,000 concurrent
connections, it has to maintain 10,000 threads

33
00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:00.760
and their memory stacks, which
quickly exhausts the machine.

34
00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:04.380
A thread pool offers a compromise
for bounded resources.

35
00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:07.580
The server creates a fixed
number of threads at startup.

36
00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:11.700
These threads act as consumers, pulling
active connections from a shared queue.

37
00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:15.910
Let's run our eight slow clients into
a bounded pool containing exactly eight

38
00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:20.060
threads. The slow clients grab
all available threads at once.

39
00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:24.250
Because they take a full second to finish
their requests, the pool reaches total

40
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:28.720
saturation. The 24 fast clients are
completely blocked from entering.

41
00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:32.610
Looking at the terminal output for the pool
model, we see the fast clients are once

42
00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:38.610
again trapped. Their median wait time spikes
to 981 milliseconds, entirely defeating

43
00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:40.780
the speed advantage of multi-threading.

44
00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:44.730
Thread pools keep a server from running out
of memory, but they fail when the number

45
00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.320
of slow clients matches
the size of the pool.

46
00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:51.680
A robust server needs an architecture
that never blocks on a slow client.

47
00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:54.220
Without spawning endless
threads to compensate.

48
00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:56.760
The solution is the event loop.

49
00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:01.110
One efficient thread handles all clients
by interacting only with actively

50
00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:06.170
transmitting sockets. Using the epoll_wait
system call, the thread asks the kernel

51
00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:09.440
which sockets have data,
jumping exclusively to those.

52
00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:14.030
By ignoring inactive connections, the thread
never wastes a millisecond waiting for

53
00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:16.660
a slow client to finish
formulating its request.

54
00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:20.040
This final block of terminal
output proves the efficiency.

55
00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:25.170
Under the exact same load, the event loop
processes the 24 fast clients in just one

56
00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:28.940
to two milliseconds, completely
unaffected by the eight slow clients.

57
00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:31.920
The event loop solves the C10K problem.

58
00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:35.360
It enables massive concurrency
with minimal system overhead.

59
00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:40.200
Let's review. What happens to a new
connection if an iterative server is busy?

60
00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.280
It waits in the socket's accept queue.

61
00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:45.140
Why does a thread pool
struggle with slow clients?

62
00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:50.860
If slow clients match the number of
threads, the pool completely saturates.

63
00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:58.360
Now open animation A8.2, server models,
in the course hub and run lab TD7.
